vlm for linux on x86_64

if you don't know what it is, you probably don't want it...

based on the admirable work of Brad Parker who changed the alpha assembler
code generator to produce c-code runnable on an x86_64 platform

this version uses a TUNTAP tap device for networking, that way
it doesn't need root privileges to run if you define a tap
interface beforehand. I decided to use a tap (as opposed to a tun)
interface because access to the complete ethernet frame is possible
with a tap interface - making my life easier since I'm currently
writing a chaosnet network module for Linux and would need access to
the complete ethernet frame therefore. Also it is possible to put tap
devices on a bridge with different mac addresses to be able to run
multiple vlm instances communicating to each other via chaosnet.

this version has fixes for:
     - X11 with xcb lib (runs with or without xcb)
     since clx directly accesses the bar-bones X11-protocol, xcb
     gets confused when you try to XCloseDisplay/XSynch because clx tries
     to keep track of the X11 calls internally which it is unable to
     do with a mixture of XLIB calls and direct access to the X11-protocol.
     This in turn keeps the vlm from cleanly closing a display, which it
     uses when terminating and/or saving a LISP world.
     So, if we detect xcb being used, we use a workaround for closing
     X displays...

     - keyboard mapping for the cold-load/debugger window
     I deemed it unsatisfactory to not being able to use the CTRL-, META-,
     SUPER-, and HYPER- keys in the cold-load/debugger window, so I did setup
     a mapping for those keys like it is done in the GENERA system itself. You
     may use the same keys now in the cold-load/debugger window.

     - rational division (/ 3) yields 1/3 instead of 0
     doesn't work in Brads version, because rational division ought to
     produce a trap if there is a non-zero remainder from a division. I've
     decide to produce asm code for rational division by generating a new
     pseudo alpha instruction (x64ratquo) and generating asm code from that.
     see alpha-assembler/imacmath.lisp and stub/process.lisp

     - double float exceptions (/ 0.0d0) results in a valid trap now
     has not been implemented in the alpha assembly version (forgotten
     perhaps).
     Changed that in the generation of alpha assembly code (tested on an alpha
     as well). see alpha-assembler/imactrap.lisp.

there are extensions for:
      - blocksize settable for attach-disk-channel (to be able to use fep-file
      images with non-2048 word blocks, like images derived from ancient
      ESDI disks.

      - a unix crypt routine
      links to unix crypt to enable use of modern crypt algorithms.

      - unix cwd and unix home dir to be found in embedded communication area

      You won't be able to use these extensions if you do not patch and
      recompile respective the LISP definitions as well.

to build:

     ./configure
     make

you need gcc, the X11 and xcb development files and libcrypt. clisp is
only needed to regenerate stub/*.c. the generic instructions are in the
file INSTALL.

WHAT IS MISSING FROM THIS REPOSITORY

this repository contains only the emulator. the emulator alone runs
nothing: Genera itself, the Lisp system it executes, is Symbolics
proprietary software and may not be distributed here. you have to
supply these files yourself:

     Genera-8-5.vlod    the Genera world (the Lisp system image)
                        required to boot
     VLM_debugger       the debugger image loaded at startup
                        required to boot
     sys.sct/           the Genera sources, fonts and on-line
                        documentation; not required to boot, but
                        without it the Document Examiner is empty

all three come from the Symbolics Open Genera 2.0 distribution (a CD
labelled OPEN_GENERA_2_0, 1998). how to find it:

     - if you own Open Genera 2.0, use your CD or an image of it.

     - Symbolics Technologies, the current rights holder, licenses
     Genera, at the time of writing free of charge for non-commercial
     use through its "Friends of Symbolics" program. see
     https://www.symbolics.biz

     - the manuals (not the software) are at
     http://bitsavers.org/pdf/symbolics/software/genera_8/
     start with the Open Genera Installation Guide and the Genera
     Workbook. copies of the manuals are in docs; docs/README
     says what to read in what order.

on the Open Genera 2.0 CD the world and the debugger are inside the
VLMBASE200 subset, and sys.sct is a directory at the top level:

     zcat AXP/OSF/VLMBASE200 | tar xf - ./usr/opt/VLM200/lib/symbolics

besides those files you need two things you create yourself:

     - a configuration file
     copy dot.VLM to .VLM in your home directory or in the directory
     you start genera from, and set genera.world and genera.debugger to
     where you put the two files above.

     - a tap network device
     genera exits at startup without one. sudo ./setup-tap.sh creates
     tap0 with the addresses used in dot.VLM. The device is gone after
     a reboot.

then start it with src/genera. genera does not exit on SIGTERM; it asks
for confirmation on its terminal instead.

./run-genera.sh does all of this in one step: it creates the tap device
and starts nfs2d.py (see below) if they are not already there, asking
for sudo only when it has to, and then starts genera. its settings
(the NFS directory, the tap device and the host address) come from
run-genera.conf; copy dot.run-genera.conf to that name and adjust it
if the defaults do not suit you.

the file NETWORKING explains what the tap device and the NFS server
are, how they fit together, and what to check when they do not work.

READING THE ON-LINE DOCUMENTATION

Genera reads sys.sct from the host over NFS version 2, which current
Linux kernels no longer serve. nfs2d.py in this directory is a small
user-space NFS version 2 server written for this purpose; it needs
only python3, and neither rpcbind nor the kernel NFS server.

the distribution world looks for the tree at /cdrom/sys.sct on the
host, so build a directory that looks like that and serve it:

     mkdir /srv/genera
     ln -s /path/to/opengenera-cd /srv/genera/cdrom
     sudo ./nfs2d.py --root /srv/genera

(/path/to/opengenera-cd is the directory that contains sys.sct.) root
is needed only for the portmapper port; the server then drops to your
own user. it listens on 10.0.0.1, the host side of tap0, and is
read-only unless you give --rw. start it after setup-tap.sh.

then, in Genera, type at the "Command:" prompt

     (si:login-to-sys-host)
     (fs:set-logical-pathname-host "SYS"
       :translations '(("**;*.*" "dis-emb-host:/cdrom/sys.sct/**/*.*")))

and press SELECT D (F1, then D) for the Document Examiner.

if the host runs a firewall, it has to let Genera's traffic in on the
tap device, or the host sees nothing but pings. with ufw:

     sudo ufw allow in on tap0 from 10.0.0.0/24

nfs2d.py also answers the time protocol. Genera asks the host for the
time at boot; if nothing answers it asks you, and then stops in the
debugger with "Unable to set calendar clock".

./configure --help will give you some configuration options
